From 43b68c2da037577545ee92b7a84288bc2dda73f0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: build <build@invalid>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 03:06:39 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] proc: serve a FEX tracee's /proc/<pid>/maps without the
 host-only mappings

A tracer reading /proc/<pid>/maps of an x86-64 tracee on a real kernel sees only
that process's x86-64 mappings: nothing can be mapped at or above the x86-64 user
address limit (TASK_SIZE_MAX, 0x7ffffffff000). When the tracee runs under FEX the
host file also lists FEX's own aarch64 binary, the host libc.so.6, ld-linux-aarch64,
libstdc++, libm and libgcc_s it links against, and its JIT, allocator and lookup
regions, all above that limit.

The current Proton Easy Anti-Cheat launcher module injects its client at the
tracee's first ld.so syscall (brk), before the guest's own libc is mapped. It
finds the library to call into by name in /proc/<pid>/maps, so the only
"libc.so.6" it sees is FEX's aarch64 one: it resolves a function there through
PTRACE_PEEKDATA, points the injected call at aarch64 code (rip 0xffffa081fc70 in
one run), the tracee faults at that address instead of at the fake return
address 9, the launcher passes the SIGSEGV on and ends with
"Launcher finished with: 210, 'Unexpected error. (#1)'". Observed with Goose
Goose Duck's EOS bootstrapper 1.9.3 on 2026-10-11. The VRChat runs of
2026-10-08 recorded an int3 at _start before the injection, i.e. after the
guest libc was mapped, which is probably why the lookup found the guest's libc
then; the current module no longer does that first.

Serve another process's /proc/<pid>/maps without the lines that start at or
above the limit when that process is a FEX tracee of the caller (its PtraceEmu
state block is reachable). Every other line is the host file's, unchanged;
nothing is fabricated. The guest's own /proc/self/maps and the maps of
processes it does not trace keep the host view.

Limits: /proc/<pid>/smaps, numa_maps and task/<tid>/maps of a tracee are not
filtered (no consumer seen); a mapping that starts below the limit and ends
above it (FEX's guest stack top) is kept as it is.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
